186526556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186526528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527110 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e29738f2-3cd0-58f4-93b4-014da2a887b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527124 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527124 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527124 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527124 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{xy-y}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & x\\ge 1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527124 #edb/ref 1074 "The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{xy-y}{x}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{y(x-1)}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527140 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527140 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527152 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527152 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})(1-{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527157 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={log}_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527157 #edb/ref 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527158 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>4 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527158 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+x\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+2y\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527159 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "449c39ae-346f-5e58-9576-63532dc73237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527159 #edb/ref 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{\\pi }{2},y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527170 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527170 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527175 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527175 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "856af2a4-8ad4-5acc-a980-a089f44a32f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527175 #edb/ref 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1){y}^{2}+(1)^{2}y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527177 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527177 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527177 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527177 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,y\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0\\le x\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527177 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527177 #edb/ref 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=F(x,\\infty )={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527190 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527190 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527190 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527190 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527190 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527190 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2+x})(1-{e}^{-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527201 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527201 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527201 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527201 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527201 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527221 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527221 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527221 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})(1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527228 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>2 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527228 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527229 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527229 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
